# Personal data — names, emails and phone numbers for AWS alternate contacts.
# Never committed. Copy config/contacts.env.example and fill in locally.
#
# Glob, not an exact match: an exact 'config/contacts.env' would leave a
# contacts.env.bak or contacts.env.old tracked. The .example file is
# re-included below because it holds only empty placeholders.
config/contacts.env*
!config/contacts.env.example

# Identity Center group membership — names and direct email addresses.
# Same rule, same reason. Copy config/identity.env.example and fill in locally.
config/identity.env*
!config/identity.env.example

# Jira account identifiers for the people who may authorise a quorum action.
# An Atlassian accountId identifies an individual, and the file also carries
# the names needed to read it, so it is personal data on both counts and gets
# the same treatment. Copy config/quorum.env.example and fill in locally.
#
# The platform reads these from SSM, never from this file directly --
# scripts/sync-quorum-approvers.sh publishes them.
config/quorum.env*
!config/quorum.env.example

# Infrastructure change approvers: Atlassian accountIds, same class of data as
# quorum.env. Published by scripts/sync-change-approvers.sh.
config/change-approvers.env*
!config/change-approvers.env.example

# Support pricing. Commercial terms, not source: a rate does not belong in git
# history, in a diff, or on a screenshare. Copy config/rates.env.example.
#
# The platform reads these from SSM, never from this file directly --
# scripts/set-cost-rates.sh publishes them, and prints the model names without
# the figures.
config/rates.env*
!config/rates.env.example

# Completed client questionnaires. The whole directory, not a filename glob.
#
# A completed questionnaire carries a client's full developer roster -- names,
# email addresses, phone numbers -- alongside their architecture. It is the
# richest single piece of personal data this repository will ever sit next to,
# and unlike config/*.env there is no predictable filename to match on: it will
# be called whatever the client is called.
#
# So the boundary is the DIRECTORY. Anything in it is assumed personal.
# README.md is re-included so the directory exists in a fresh clone and
# explains itself -- an ignored directory that is not in the repository is
# invisible, and somebody will put a completed form next to the blank template
# instead.
completed_qs/*
!completed_qs/README.md

# Local AWS and tooling state
.aws-sam/
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
.terraform/
.terraform.lock.hcl.bak

# Completed onboarding questionnaires carry named contacts with direct email
# addresses — technical, escalation, change approver, billing, developer
# manager. Same class of data as config/contacts.env and the same rule.
#
# The example and the schema are placeholders and structure only, and are
# re-included below.
*.questionnaire.yaml
*.questionnaire.yml
*.questionnaire.json
!questionnaire.example.yaml
!questionnaire.schema.json

# Developer rosters — questionnaire section 3b, held as a sidecar beside the
# completed questionnaire. A roster is nothing BUT personal data: one row per
# person, with name, email and which environments they reach.
#
# completed_qs/* already covers the intended location. This covers the
# unintended ones — a roster saved at the repository root or next to the
# template while somebody is filling it in, which is exactly when it is most
# likely to be staged by accident.
*.roster.yaml
*.roster.yml
*.roster.json
!roster.example.yaml
!roster.schema.json

# Terraform variable files hold responder email addresses — personal data by
# the same rule as config/contacts.env. Same glob-not-exact-match reasoning:
# a terraform.tfvars.bak must not survive the rule. The .example file holds
# placeholders only and is re-included.
*.tfvars
*.tfvars.json
!*.tfvars.example

# Editor / OS
.vscode/
.idea/
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
*.swp

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/
venv/

# Build output
dist/
build/
node_modules/

# Scratch
*.log
tmp/
scratch/

# Build artifact: validate_catalog.py emits it from runbooks/catalog.yaml at
# packaging. Committing it would let a stale copy ship if the YAML changed and
# packaging was skipped — the catalog has one source and it is the YAML.
runbooks/src/catalog.json

# Build artifact: scripts/lib/extract_event_specs.py generates it from 06a at
# packaging. A committed copy could ship stale if 06a changed and packaging was
# skipped, and the router would then not recognise an event class it forwards.
alerting/src/event_specs.json
